timestamp=2026-11-04T12:04:29.873Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Look for more clues\n# Maybe patients who have \"bereavement\" type claims are the ones who suffered loss? Or the deceased is someone else.\n# Another angle: sometimes in insurance data, if the insured is deceased, the record might show certain patterns - e.g. coverage status changes, or claims filed by others? Or maybe the patient_id of the deceased appears in contexts.\n\n# Also look at the three death-related claims again:\n# 1. PAT_033 has bereavement compensation, contact is PAT_011. Also look at PAT_033's own record - they have a claim (bereavement). That means PAT_033 is the claimant. So if claimant files bereavement, maybe a relative died. Or maybe PAT_033 is the one who died? Unusual to have a \"patient\" record for a deceased person still active? Or maybe not.\n\n# 2. PAT_265 has family support adjustment, contact PAT_065. PAT_265's own coverage is inactive? \"coverage_status\" is inactive for PAT_265. That could indicate death (policy terminated due to death). Also large payment. And emergency contact PAT_065 - PAT_065 has no claims, no coverage status fields filled? Look at their row: PAT_065 appears as patient_id with empty fields (see earlier: PAT_065 has no compensation data). That could mean they are just listed as contact, maybe not a patient in treatment.\n\n# 3. PAT_006 has life event claim, contact PAT_078. Both are active patients with claims? PAT_078 has property damage claim, active. PAT_006 is active and filed life event claim.\n\n# Also \"family support adjustment\" after death - often if primary beneficiary is a minor/dependent, or support is adjusted. Or maybe the patient (PAT_265) died, and family support is being adjusted for dependents. In that case, PAT_265 might be the deceased.\n\n# Similarly, bereavement compensation - typically paid to surviving family. If PAT_033 files bereavement, then maybe someone related to PAT_033 died. Who? Look at their emergency contacts and who they are contact for.\n# PAT_033's emergency contact is PAT_011. Also PAT_033 is emergency contact for no one? Wait look up: listed as contact for who? PAT_033 is emergency contact for... let us check: search for rows where emergency_contact_patient_id == PAT_033? Let us look back at reverse map.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
