timestamp=2026-11-04T12:04:47.377Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# PAT_033 has bereavement compensation. And several people list PAT_033 as their emergency contact (PAT_129, PAT_221, PAT_069 all have active claims and list PAT_033 as contact; PAT_174 lists PAT_033 as contact but has no claims).\n# That pattern often means PAT_033 is the policy holder/parent/guardian who is deceased - so dependents list them as emergency contact? Or alternatively, if the patient is a child/minor, their parent is emergency contact. But then if parent dies, bereavement is filed?\n# Or maybe PAT_033 is the deceased. That would explain bereavement claim filed under PAT_033's name/record? Or filed by someone else using PAT_033's info? Or the \"patient_id\" on the bereavement claim row is the deceased's ID.\n# Another interpretation: the deceased is the subject. If a patient (deceased) had a policy, sometimes claims like bereavement/final expenses are processed. Or the claimant is the emergency contact.\n\n# Also look up PAT_174 more - they have no claims, maybe a minor/dependent?\nfor pid in ['PAT_174']:\n    for r in rows:\n        if r['patient_id'] == pid:\n            print(r)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
