timestamp=2026-11-04T12:05:57.745Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Another case: PAT_065 is contact for PAT_265, PAT_178, PAT_146. PAT_265 has family support adjustment, inactive. Maybe PAT_065 died - and family support is being arranged. Or maybe PAT_265 died? \n# Also PAT_265 is the one filing the family support adjustment claim. If the patient themselves files family support adjustment after their own death - no. Usually filed by survivors. Or the claim is processed post-death. Also PAT_265's coverage is inactive. That strongly suggests PAT_265 is deceased.\n\n# That would mean PAT_065 (their emergency contact) is likely a surviving relative? Or could be the other way.\n# Similarly, if a child is deceased, parent is emergency contact and bereavement might be filed. But the claim record - whose patient_id is on it?\n\n# Also look at the amounts: bereavement compensation 102700 to PAT_033, family support adjustment 101400 to PAT_265. Similar amounts - both large settlements. And life event claim 98300 to PAT_006. Also large.\n# These are likely death-related settlements. The \"patient_id\" on the claim might be the claimant or the deceased? \n# Another common pattern: if the insured person dies, their record may show as inactive, and beneficiaries file. Or sometimes the claim is under the policy holder's ID.\n# But also look at who else is connected. For example, dependents of PAT_033 (who has bereavement) list PAT_033 as contact - if PAT_033 died, the dependents are orphans/need new guardian. That fits.\n\n# Similarly, PAT_265 has inactive status + family support adjustment. Maybe PAT_265 is the deceased. Who are they? And who depends on them? Or who do they depend on?\n# PAT_265 lists PAT_065 as emergency contact. And PAT_065 is contact for PAT_178, PAT_146 also. Maybe PAT_065 is the parent, and PAT_265, PAT_178, PAT_146 are their children/dependents. If something happens to one of them (PAT_265), family support adjustment is filed. Or if PAT_065 (parent) dies, then family support might be needed. Hmm.\n\n# Also look at ages/names? Names are just strings. No age. Hard to tell. Another angle: look at all the people who have no claims themselves (empty compensation fields) - many of them are listed as emergency contacts. Also some have no coverage data at all (like PAT_065, PAT_174 etc). That often means they’re not active patients/treatment cases, just contacts in the system. That suggests they might be external contacts.\n# Also look at the question: \"three patient IDs that are most likely to be deceased\"\n# So the answer should be patient IDs (from the dataset). Likely the ones whose deaths triggered bereavement/family support/life event claims, and/or have inactive status with death-related claims.\n\n# Best candidates: \n# - PAT_265: family support adjustment claim, coverage_status inactive. That’s a strong indicator of death.\n# - Also check others with inactive status? There are 7 with inactive (from earlier). Let us look at all of them.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
