timestamp=2026-11-04T12:06:37.151Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Look at PAT_265 - only one with death-related family support + inactive + large payment. That’s definitely deceased.\n# What about the others with inactive? They have normal injury claims, smaller-ish payments. Inactive could be due to policy cancellation, non-payment, etc. - not necessarily death.\n\n# What about PAT_033 who has bereavement compensation? Their coverage status is active. That’s odd if they are deceased - but maybe policy still active for a period, or record not updated. Or maybe the bereavement is filed by the estate/claimant under their record? Or maybe PAT_033 is not the deceased but the claimant (e.g. spouse filing bereavement for deceased partner). Who died then?\n# Also who are the dependents of PAT_033? The people listing PAT_033 as contact: PAT_129, PAT_221, PAT_174, PAT_069. If PAT_033 (adult) died, these might be children/spouse? And the bereavement compensation goes to them. That suggests PAT_033 is the deceased.\n\n# Similarly for family support: if the primary provider dies, family support adjustment happens. Is PAT_265 the provider or the dependent?\n# PAT_265 lists PAT_065 as contact. So PAT_065 is likely the primary. And PAT_265 is possibly a child/dependent? They have their own record and even had a claim (family support adjustment claim is filed under PAT_265?). The claim is filed as \"family support adjustment\" for PAT_265. Also \"coverage_status\" is inactive for PAT_265. That could mean the dependent (PAT_265) passed away? Or it could mean the policy/coverage for them ended due to the primary’s death. Unclear.\n\n# Another case: PAT_006 has life event claim, active status. Also connected via PAT_078 and PAT_011. Life event claims can be for death of family member. If a child (PAT_006) files life event claim because a parent (PAT_078 or PAT_011) died, maybe? Or vice versa.\n\n# Also look up PAT_011 - who is PAT_011? They are emergency contact for PAT_078, PAT_033, PAT_167. Also PAT_078’s contact is PAT_011, and PAT_078 has a property damage claim (active). PAT_011 themselves have a minor claim and active. If PAT_011 died, you’d expect some bereavement-related claim somewhere tied to them. But we only see bereavement for PAT_033, family support for PAT_265, life event for PAT_006. No direct bereavement claim where PAT_011 is the obvious deceased.\n\n# Also consider the three people with death-related claim types as the claimants - but the question asks for \"patient IDs that are most likely to be deceased\". The claimants might not be deceased; the deceased might be someone else. But in the data, the \"patient_id\" is the key field for each record.\n# Maybe the deceased are the ones who appear as emergency contacts for many dependents AND have death-related claims associated? Or look for patterns.\n# For example, PAT_033: has bereavement claim, is listed as emergency contact by 4 others (some with no claims, suggesting dependents). That’s plausible that PAT_033 is deceased.\n# PAT_065: listed as emergency contact by 3 (including PAT_265 who has family support adjustment, inactive). Also PAT_065 themselves have no claims and sparse data. Maybe PAT_065 is deceased?\n# PAT_265: has family support adjustment, inactive coverage. Also listed as dependent of PAT_065. Could PAT_265 be deceased? That’s possible too - family support adjustment after a death, and coverage becomes inactive.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
