timestamp=2026-11-04T12:08:11.414Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Build maps\n# Who is likely deceased? Common answers for such puzzles:\n# - The patient whose coverage is inactive AND has a death/family support claim? PAT_265 fits.\n# - The emergency contact who has many dependents and there's a bereavement claim filed by someone who lists them as contact? Or sometimes the patient_id on bereavement claim is the deceased. Also look up how others interpret similar patterns.\n\n# Another interpretation: the deceased are individuals who are listed as emergency contacts but have no record of being alive (no claims, no active coverage data) and there is a death-related payout. For example, if someone dies, their emergency contact might be an existing patient? Or not.\n\n# Also look at the dependents: for PAT_033 (bereavement claimant, contact is PAT_011), the dependents are those who list PAT_033 as contact (PAT_129,221,174,069). If PAT_033 is the deceased, then PAT_033 is the patient who died. That’s plausible.\n# Similarly, PAT_265 has family support adjustment - maybe PAT_265 is the deceased (child/spouse). Their contact is PAT_065 (parent/spouse). And there’s family support adjustment. Also coverage inactive. That fits deceased patient.\n# PAT_006 has life event claim - life event could be death in family. If PAT_006’s life event is due to death of someone close (e.g. PAT_078 or PAT_011), then the deceased might be PAT_078 or PAT_011.\n\n# Look at the structure: \"bereavement compensation\" claim type is usually paid when someone dies. Who is the decedent? The policy holder? Or a covered family member? If filed under PAT_033's record as the patient with that claim, maybe PAT_033 is the claimant receiving bereavement, but the deceased is someone else. Or sometimes in medical billing contexts, the \"patient\" on the claim is the person who received services - but bereavement is not a service. Hmm.\n\n# Another angle: look up common insurance terms. \"Family support adjustment\" often occurs after death of the primary earner. \"Bereavement compensation\" paid to family after death. \"Life event claim\" can include death, marriage, birth etc.\n# Also look at other clues: inactive coverage + large payout is common for deceased. PAT_265 is inactive with large payout and family support adjustment - strong case for deceased.\n# Others with inactive have normal claims and smaller payouts - more likely policy lapsed.\n\n# For PAT_033: active coverage, large bereavement payout. Maybe the deceased is someone else (e.g. a dependent of PAT_033 died)? Or maybe PAT_033 is the deceased but system hasn't marked coverage inactive yet? Or maybe the bereavement compensation is paid to PAT_033 as survivor.\n\n# And who are the people who list PAT_033 as emergency contact? They seem to be young-ish names (Rowan, etc.), some have no claims. Could be children. If PAT_033 is the parent and the other parent (PAT_011?) is involved? PAT_033 lists PAT_011 as emergency contact. So maybe PAT_033 and PAT_011 are partners, and children list PAT_033 as contact (or maybe either). If PAT_033 died, then children would need support - bereavement compensation might be paid. Also family support might come into play. That suggests PAT_033 is deceased.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
