timestamp=2026-11-04T12:09:00.075Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Now look at these no-claim patients who are contacts for others. \n# Also check if any of them have inactive coverage? (a person record might have coverage_status)\n# Or if they appear as claimants in death-related claims? No - we saw the death-related claims are filed by PAT_265, PAT_033, PAT_006 themselves.\n\n# Also look at the dependents of people with death-related claims more closely.\n# For PAT_033 (bereavement): dependents are PAT_129, PAT_221, PAT_174, PAT_069. All of them have active claims/status. That’s normal if they’re alive and receiving care. But PAT_033 is their emergency contact - if PAT_033 died, the contact field might be stale? Or updated to someone else? But in the dataset, it still says PAT_033. That suggests PAT_033 is no longer alive.\n# That makes PAT_033 a likely deceased patient.\n\n# For PAT_265 (family support adjustment, inactive): their contact is PAT_065. Also PAT_065 is contact for PAT_178, PAT_146 too. So PAT_065 is likely the parent/guardian of PAT_265 (and possibly others). If PAT_265 died (inactive + family support claim), then PAT_265 is the deceased. Also the family support adjustment is for the family after PAT_265’s death? Or for dependents? The claim is filed under PAT_265’s patient record. That points to PAT_265 being the deceased.\n# Also PAT_065 (parent) has no claims - maybe PAT_065 is also deceased? Let us look up: is there any bereavement/family support claim involving PAT_065? No direct. But they have 3 dependents and no claims themselves - could be elderly or deceased. Also they have no coverage status data. That’s suspicious.\n\n# Similarly, PAT_084 has 3 dependents but no claims themselves - no coverage data either? Let us check.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
