timestamp=2026-11-04T12:10:07.586Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# PAT_265 has family support adjustment, inactive coverage. Also 4 people list PAT_265 as their emergency contact (PAT_023, 034, 175, 192). And PAT_265 themselves list PAT_065 as contact.\n# That structure is odd if PAT_265 is the deceased. Or if PAT_265 is the primary policy holder/guardian, then others list them as contact. And if PAT_265 dies, family support adjustment is filed under PAT_265's record, and coverage becomes inactive. That fits.\n# Also the dependents (those listing PAT_265 as contact) might need family support after PAT_265's death. That’s plausible. So PAT_265 is likely the deceased.\n\n# Now for PAT_033: they have bereavement compensation. They list PAT_011 as contact. Others (PAT_129, 221, 174, 069) list PAT_033 as contact. So PAT_033 is likely the primary adult. If PAT_033 dies, dependents list PAT_033 as contact and bereavement is paid. That fits PAT_033 being deceased.\n\n# For PAT_006: they have life event claim, active. They list PAT_078 as contact. Only PAT_006 lists PAT_078 as contact (PAT_078 is contact only for PAT_006). Also PAT_078 lists PAT_011 as contact. And others list PAT_011 as contact. Is there a death in the family chain?\n# Life event claim can be triggered by death of a close family member. Also large payout. Maybe PAT_078 (parent/guardian of PAT_006) died? That would be a life event for PAT_006. Also PAT_078 has their own active claims (property damage) and is active. If PAT_078 died, would they still be \"active\" with other claim types? Or maybe PAT_011 (grandparent/other relation) died? Since PAT_078 lists PAT_011 as contact, and PAT_011 is contact for others too - maybe PAT_011 is the deceased, causing life event for PAT_006? Let us check all three cases together.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
