timestamp=2026-11-04T12:10:23.153Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Look at the full family trees\ndef get_record(pid):\n    for r in rows:\n        if r['patient_id'] == pid:\n            return r\n    return None\n\n# Case 1: PAT_033 (bereavement) - lists PAT_011 as contact. Dependents of PAT_033: PAT_129,221,174,069.\n# Case 2: PAT_265 (family support, inactive) - lists PAT_065 as contact. Dependents of PAT_265: PAT_023,034,175,192.\n# Case 3: PAT_006 (life event) - lists PAT_078 as contact. PAT_078 lists PAT_011 as contact.\n# Also PAT_011 is contact for PAT_033, PAT_078, PAT_167. And others list PAT_265 as contact... \n\n# Is there a common pattern? Another possibility is that the deceased are those with no claims AND are emergency contacts for many AND possibly have \"inactive\" coverage or just missing data? For example, PAT_065 has no claims, is contact for 3, has no coverage data - could be deceased.\n# Also PAT_084 has 3 dependents, no claims, no coverage data - could be deceased.\n# Similarly PAT_218, PAT_126, PAT_203, PAT_267 (all contacts, no claims, sparse data, each contacted by >=3). These are all \"no claim\" patients but are heavily listed as contacts. That could mean they were policy holders/guardians who passed away - so others list them as contact but they themselves don't generate new claims. That’s plausible.\n\n# But the question says \"three patient IDs that are most likely to be deceased\". We need to pick 3. Let us look at the specific death-related claims again. \n# The death-related claims are filed by: PAT_265 (family support), PAT_033 (bereavement), PAT_006 (life event). \n# Sometimes in these datasets, the \"patient\" in the death-related claim context is the deceased? Or the claimant. Another interpretation: the patient_id is the deceased person. For example, life insurance pays out on death of the insured - the insured is the deceased. \n# That would mean PAT_006 themselves is the deceased (life event claim on their policy)? Or maybe PAT_006 is the beneficiary filing. But it's recorded as a claim under PAT_006's patient record with reason \"life event claim\" and large payment - this is a bit ambiguous.\n\n# Also look at how the data is structured: all rows are \"patients\" in the registry. Another common pattern: when someone dies, you might see a bereavement claim and the patient marked differently. Or the deceased might appear as rows with certain claim types.\n# Another possibility is that the three deceased are the emergency contacts who have no claims and are listed by many, and whose \"removal\" or death triggered claims. For example, PAT_065 - no claims, 3 dependents including PAT_265 who has family support adjustment. That suggests PAT_065 died, causing family support adjustment for PAT_265. So PAT_065 is deceased.\n# Similarly, if PAT_033 is the emergency contact for others and there is bereavement claim filed by someone (maybe PAT_011 or others?), and also PAT_033 has \"bereavement compensation\" claim themselves? That’s confusing. Or maybe the bereavement compensation is for the death of a dependent - but the row is under the claimant.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
