timestamp=2026-11-04T12:11:18.203Z level=INFO run=282380a7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Look up who might be filing. Bereavement compensation is usually paid to surviving spouse/child/parent. \n# For PAT_033's bereavement claim: PAT_033's emergency contact is PAT_011. And PAT_011 is active, has their own claim. Also PAT_033 is listed as contact by children/dependents. That suggests PAT_033 is the deceased parent, and PAT_011 might be the surviving spouse/other parent. That fits PAT_033 as deceased.\n\n# For PAT_265's family support adjustment (inactive): they list PAT_065 as contact. PAT_065 has no claims, no coverage data, 3 dependents total. That suggests PAT_065 might be the deceased parent/guardian, and PAT_265 is a surviving dependent receiving family support? Or is PAT_265 the deceased?\n# Also \"family support adjustment\" after death of primary earner - the primary earner’s death triggers support for dependents. So primary earner (PAT_065) might be deceased. That makes PAT_065 deceased. And PAT_265 is the dependent receiving support.\n# That also explains why PAT_265 has the family support adjustment claim under their name and is marked inactive? Or marked inactive because they’re a minor dependent under a policy that changed? Or sometimes dependents’ coverage status changes. Or another interpretation: PAT_265 is the deceased child, and family support is adjusted for the family. That’s possible too.\n\n# Also look at the other dependents of PAT_065: PAT_178 and PAT_146 have no claims, no coverage data. They’re just listed. That’s consistent with being minors/dependents. If PAT_065 (parent) died, family support would be needed for all dependents. That fits.\n# Compare to PAT_033’s dependents: they do have active claims - they’re getting treatment, older perhaps. Different situation.\n\n# Another hint: look at the emergency contact relationships. If the patient is a child, emergency contact is usually parent. If the patient is an adult, emergency contact might be spouse/partner. \n# PAT_033 lists PAT_011 as contact - both are adults with claims. That suggests they’re partners. If one dies (PAT_033), the other (PAT_011) survives. Bereavement compensation goes to survivor. That fits PAT_033 being deceased.\n# PAT_265 lists PAT_065 as contact - PAT_065 has no other info, could be an older parent/guardian. If PAT_065 dies, then PAT_265 (and other dependents) receive family support. That fits PAT_065 being deceased.\n\n# So for the three death-related claim cases:\n1. Bereavement compensation claim under PAT_033’s record → likely PAT_033 is the deceased (adult), survivor is PAT_011.\n2. Family support adjustment claim under PAT_265’s record, and PAT_265 is a dependent of PAT_065 → likely PAT_065 (primary guardian) is the deceased, triggering support for dependents including PAT_265.\n3. Life event claim under PAT_006 → what triggered it? PAT_006 lists PAT_078 as contact. PAT_078 lists PAT_011 as contact. And PAT_011 is also contact for PAT_033 (who is deceased in case 1). Is there a connection? Maybe PAT_011 is also deceased? That would be another death in the network. Also PAT_011 is contact for PAT_078 and PAT_167. If PAT_011 died, that’s a life event for connected family members. PAT_006’s life event claim could be due to PAT_011’s death? That would make PAT_011 the deceased.\n\n# That gives us three candidates: PAT_033, PAT_065, PAT_011 (or maybe PAT_078 instead). Which three are most likely?\n# Also check PAT_011’s status - they are active with their own claim. If they’re deceased, that’s odd. But maybe not updated yet. Or maybe PAT_078 died instead? PAT_078 is active too. Both have active coverage and claims.\n# Or PAT_265 themselves? PAT_265 is inactive with family support claim - that also points to PAT_265 being deceased. And the family support is for surviving family members. That’s possible too.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
